{"path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment1.md","content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: comment overstates the dispatched probe's coverage**

The `hasRecentHeadRun` doc comment claims a completed run with an unparseable timestamp is still covered because "the 15-min completed grace of the dispatched probe covers it if it is CI":

```js
// ... A completed run
// with no parseable timestamp never blocks (cannot be aged — the 15-min
// completed grace of the dispatched probe covers it if it is CI).
```

That's not accurate: `hasActiveDispatchedRun` ages completed runs with `new Date(r.updated_at || r.created_at).getTime()`, so an unparseable/missing timestamp yields `NaN`, and `age >= 0 && age < 15*60*1000` is false — the dispatched probe fails to block that run too. The observable behavior (never blocks) is identical in both probes; only the parenthetical justification is wrong.

Suggested rewording:

```js
// ... A completed run with no parseable timestamp never blocks — neither
// probe can age it (this probe and hasActiveDispatchedRun both fail OPEN
// on unageable runs).
```

Comment-only, no behavior change.
"}

{"path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment3.md","content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: active-status list duplicates `hasActiveHeadRun`'s set**

The active-status check in `hasRecentHeadRun` re-lists the same status vocabulary as the adjacent `hasActiveHeadRun` (plus `'requested'`, which is a deliberate widening):

```js
if (r.status === 'queued' || r.status === 'in_progress' ||
    r.status === 'waiting' || r.status === 'pending' ||
    r.status === 'requested') return true;
```

Two copies of the "what counts as an active run" list in the same file can drift (e.g., GitHub adds a status, one is updated, the other isn't). Consider a tiny shared predicate so the vocabulary lives in one place, e.g.:

```js
function isActiveRunStatus(s) {
    return s === 'queued' || s === 'in_progress' ||
        s === 'waiting' || s === 'pending' || s === 'requested';
}
```

and have both `hasActiveHeadRun` and `hasRecentHeadRun` delegate to it. Optional polish — the current duplication is five lines.
"}

